<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 03: Newton's laws</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers section 2.9
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
         		 Look at the free-body diagrams on page 66 of the text. Why is the force <m>F_2</m> labeled like that
                 and not as <m>W_a</m>? Can you think of a situation where <m>F_2</m> and <m>W_a</m> 
                 have different magnitudes? Describe briefly.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	 On example 2.5.1, the book used the coordinate system shown on the left to describe the position of the
             block and wedge. Why not use the coordinate system on the right, which incorporates the 
             <q>constraint that the block slides on the wedge</q> more naturally?
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
            <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz3/wedge-conventional.svg">
                <shortdescription>
                  A triangular wedge lies on a horizontal surface. A block
                  lies on top of the wedge.
                </shortdescription>
                <description>
                  <p>
                    A triangular wedge lies on a horizontal surface. Its position is
                    described by the distance <m>X</m> between the wedge and a vertical wall.
                    The height <m>h</m> of the wedge is also labeled.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    A block lies on top of the wedge. Its position is described by the distance
                    <m>x'</m> between the block and the vertical wall, as well as its height
                    <m>y'</m> above the ground.
                  </p>
                </description>
            </image>

            <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz3/wedge-tilted.svg">
                <shortdescription>Same image as the one on the left,
                  using a different coordinate system.
                </shortdescription>
                  <description>
                  <p>
                    A triangular wedge lies on a horizontal surface. Its position is
                    described by the distance <m>X</m> between the wedge and a vertical wall.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    A block lies on top of the wedge. Its position is described using coordinates
                    <m>x',y'</m>. The coordinate <m>x'</m> is the distance between the block and the top of the 
                    wedge, measured along the wedge.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    The coordinate <m>y'</m> is the distance between the block and the surface of the
                    wedge, measured perpendicularly to the wedge.
                  </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Draw the forces acting on the combined pulley-and-block system on example 2.5.2
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A table rests on the floor. A <alert>tightly stretched spring</alert> is connected between the 
                table and the floor, as shown below. Draw the force diagram for the table.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider a car at rest. Which of Newton's laws lets us conclude that the downward 
                gravitational pull of the Earth on the car and the upward contact force of the ground on the car 
                are equal and opposite?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>